#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 4.6.28
# DESCRIPTION
# Given a position function s(t), find:
#    (a) The velocity and acceleration functions
#    (b) When velocity is positive/negative
#    (c) When acceleration is positive/negative
#    (d) The times when the particle is speeding up/slowing down

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Rates of change - engineering and physics)
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('4.6')
## Problem1('28')
## KEYWORDS('Functions', 'Applied Problems', 'Velocity', 'Acceleration', 'Speed', 'Position')
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

#    s(t) = t(t - A)^2 + B
Context("Numeric")->variables->add(t => 'Real');
$A = random(1, 9, 2);
$B = random(1, 10);

$Posfunc = Formula("t^3 - 2*$A t^2 + $A^2 t + $B")->reduce;

#   (a) Velocity & Acceleration functions
$Velfunc = Formula("3t^2 - 4*$A t + $A^2")->reduce;
$Accfunc = Formula("6t - 4*$A")->reduce;

#   (b,c) When is velocity positive? negative?
Context("Fraction");
$Endpt1 = Formula("$A/3")->reduce;
$Endpt2 = Formula("2*$A/3")->reduce;

Context("Interval");
$Velposint = Interval("[0,$Endpt1) U ($A,inf)");
$Velnegint = Interval("($Endpt1, $A)");

#  (d,e) When is acceleration positive? negative?
$Accposint = Interval("($Endpt2,inf)");
$Accnegint = Interval("[0,$Endpt2)");

#   (f) Times when particle is speeding up/slowing down
#       -  Velocity and acceleration must be same sign
$Speedup  = Interval("($Endpt1,$Endpt2) U ($A,inf)");
$Slowdown = Interval("[0,$Endpt1) U ($Endpt2,$A)");

Context("Numeric")->variables->add(t => 'Real');
$Accfactored = Formula("2(3t - 2*$A)")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML
The function [` s(t)`] describes the position of a particle moving along 
a coordinate line, where [`s`] is in feet and [` t `] is in seconds.
[``` s(t) = [$Posfunc], \qquad t \ge 0 ```]

a) Find the velocity and acceleration functions.

    [# [. [`v(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Velfunc} .]*
     [. [`a(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Accfunc} .]* 
     #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

b) Over what interval(s) is the particle moving in the positive direction?

    [_]{$Velposint}

c) Over what interval(s) is the particle moving in the negative direction?

    [_]{$Velnegint}

d) Over what interval(s) does the particle have positive acceleration?

    [_]{$Accposint}

e) Over what interval(s) does the particle have negative acceleration?

    [_]{$Accnegint}

f) Over what interval is the particle speeding up? Slowing down?

    [# [. Speeding Up:   .] [. [_]{$Speedup} .]*
      [.  Slowing Down: .] [. [_]{$Slowdown} .]*
    #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

[@ helpLink('interval') @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) To find the velocity function, take the derivative of the position function, [` s(t) `]. 
[``` s\;'(t) = v(t) = [$Velfunc].```]
    To find the acceleration function, take the derivative of the velocity function.
[``` v\;'(t) = a(t) = [$Accfunc].```]
    Note that this is also the second derivative of the position function.

b) The particle will be moving in the positive direction when its velocity is greater than 0.
[```[$Velfunc] > 0 \quad \Rightarrow (3t - [$A])(t - [$A]) > 0```]
This inequality is satisfied over [`\left[0,[$Endpt1]\right) \cup \left([$A],\infty\right)`].

c) The particle will be moving in the negative direction when its velocity is less than 0.
[```[$Velfunc] < 0 \quad \Rightarrow (3t - [$A])(t - [$A]) < 0```]
This inequality is satisfied over [`\left([$Endpt1],[$A]\right)`].

d) The particle will have positive acceleration when [`a(t) > 0`].
[```[$Accfunc] > 0 \quad \Rightarrow [$Accfactored] > 0 \quad \Rightarrow t > [$Endpt2]```]
This is the interval [`\left([$Endpt2],\infty\right)`].

e) The particle will have negative acceleration when [`a(t) < 0`].
[```[$Accfunc] < 0 \quad \Rightarrow [$Accfactored] < 0 \quad \Rightarrow t < [$Endpt2]```]
This is the interval [`\left[0,[$Endpt2]\right)`].

f) To determine when the particle is speeding up or slowing down, we need to look at the signs of the
velocity and acceleration:
    - If the velocity is positive, then a positive acceleration 
will cause it to speed up
    - If the velocity is negative, then a negative acceleration
will cause it to speed up
Therefore, the particle is speeding up whenever the velocity and acceleration are of the same sign.

    *Speeding Up*
      
    The velocity is positive over [`\left[0,[$Endpt1]\right) \cup \left([$A],\infty\right)`], while the
    acceleration is positive over [`\left([$Endpt2],\infty\right)`]. These intervals coincide over the
    interval [`\left([$A],\infty\right)`]. 
    
    The velocity is negative over [`\left([$Endpt1],[$A]\right)`], while the
    acceleration is negative over [`\left[0,[$Endpt2]\right)`]. These intervals coincide over the
    interval [`\left([$Endpt1],[$Endpt2]\right)`].
    
    Therefore, the particle is speeding up over [`\left([$Endpt1],[$Endpt2]\right) \cup \left([$A],\infty\right)`]. 
    
    *Slowing Down*
      
    Since the particle is speeding up over [`\left([$Endpt1],[$Endpt2]\right) \cup \left([$A],\infty\right)`],
    the particle must be slowing down over [`\left[0,[$Endpt1]\right) \cup \left([$Endpt2],[$A]\right)`]. 

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
